## Braintrust compares MCP tool requests with the results agents received

Source: Braintrust — https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/mcp-tracing
Published: 2026-11-05
Channel: news

Braintrust is an AI observability and evaluation platform that records agent activity for inspection. Its new server tracing for Model Context Protocol, the interface agents use to call tools, addresses a visibility gap: a server operator may see a successful response without knowing that the tool returned the wrong data. The operator may also lack the agent’s full conversation.

First, enable tracing with the Braintrust JavaScript or Python SDK; the announcement covers official MCP implementations and FastMCP. Each call records its inputs, results, timing and errors. Then open the call in Logs and compare the request with the response. The article illustrates this with a production-only query that incorrectly returns staging records.

Operators can optionally add telemetry.intent to the tool schema and a send_feedback tool to collect an agent’s stated purpose and reported difficulties. These additions are not prerequisites for tracing. Patterns can surface repeated behavior with supporting calls. After a repair, the article recommends repeating the request, adding a test and reviewing later traces for recurrence.

Why it matters: MCP server owners can investigate successful calls whose content was wrong. Treat agent-supplied intent and feedback as clues to check against recorded inputs and results.

