## Elastic’s agent traces show a cheaper model can still answer more slowly

Source: Elastic Observability Labs — https://www.elastic.co/observability-labs/blog/ai-agent-observability-opentelemetry
Published: 2026-11-05
Channel: news

Elastic Observability collects application telemetry for investigating behavior, latency and cost. Its walkthrough instruments a LangChain shopping assistant with OpenTelemetry’s generative-AI conventions, shared names for model and agent records. The problem is that total request time alone cannot identify which model call made the customer wait or consumed the budget.

First, the example connects agent, tool and model operations as spans, each recording one operation and its timing. A trace shows three model calls taking about 92% of one request. The application separately collects provider-reported model charges and puts their sum on the request’s root record; this excludes infrastructure and retrieval charges. Parent and child durations must not be added together.

Then the author replaces only the product-comparison model and tests five questions three times per model, checking answers as well as cost and latency. Model spend falls by 76% to 83% per question, but mean response time rises on four questions. This is a small sequential test, not a production guarantee. The article recommends broader evaluation and testing whether returning the tool’s answer directly can avoid the final model call.

Why it matters: Measure answer quality, elapsed time and model charges together before adopting a cheaper model. Use traces to choose the next experiment, then repeat the same checks.

