## Langfuse records more trace reads, exports and downloads in access audits

Source: Langfuse — https://github.com/langfuse/langfuse/releases/tag/v4.76.0
Published: 2026-11-05
Channel: github

Langfuse is an AI observability platform for inspecting application traces and evaluation scores. Those records can contain sensitive application data, so reviewing who accessed them is a separate operational need from collecting them. The release expands audit access events for selected reads of trace, observation, session and score data, and for exports and downloads. An observation records an operation within a trace.

First, an organization must have Enterprise access-event recording enabled, either through its organization setting or instance enforcement. Covered reads declare the resource and action beside their handler. The implementation records validated request parameters before returning data; a staging-write failure prevents a successful read from releasing it. Selected refused reads also receive denied events.

Exports record before their jobs enter the queue, while covered downloads record before sending bytes. A failed audit write prevents those operations from proceeding. Coverage remains selective: aggregate-only reads and several other routes are excluded. Scheduled exports are outside the export change, batch-export row counts are unavailable at request time, and refused downloads are not recorded as denied. These boundaries come from the linked implementation notes.

Why it matters: Teams using Langfuse access audits should test the specific read and export paths they depend on; the expanded coverage does not establish a complete record of every access.

