## OpenInference removes duplicate Anthropic SDK spans from traced calls

Source: Arize AI / OpenInference — https://github.com/Arize-ai/openinference/releases/tag/%40arizeai/openinference-instrumentation-anthropic%401.0.1
Published: 2026-11-05
Channel: github

OpenInference is an instrumentation library that records AI model operations in traces. Its JavaScript Anthropic integration fixes duplicate records introduced when the Anthropic SDK began creating its own span for every API call. A span describes one operation within a trace. Instrumented calls consequently exported both an OpenInference model span and an SDK span for the same request.

The release identifies message creation, message streaming and token counting as affected, across stable and beta interfaces. Streaming had an additional hierarchy problem: its SDK span was not beneath the OpenInference model span. The patch changes the context the integration hands to the SDK when a call is already traced.

First, OpenInference supplies a non-recording span carrying the model span’s context. The SDK then retains that context without exporting a second record. The request continues to carry trace context, and HTTP instrumentation records sit beneath the model operation. Calls that OpenInference does not trace keep the SDK’s own spans. This is a targeted correction to record duplication and nesting; the release does not report a measured reduction in telemetry cost or changes to model behavior.

Why it matters: JavaScript teams combining Anthropic’s SDK with OpenInference should inspect a streamed request after upgrading and confirm one model record with the expected HTTP children. The earlier Python fix is a separate integration.

